for today's classic interview we are  going to an interview I did a couple of  years ago about two and a half years ago  with mark Hyden and mark Hyden is  against the death penalty but what was  interesting about mark Hyden is that  he's part of a group called  conservatives against the death penalty  and I thought it'd be interesting to  check out that interview especially now  in the context of Donald Trump talking  about wanting to execute drug dealers  and actually expand use of the death  penalty also in the context of all the  problem problems that many states have  been having getting the drugs that are  often used in these lethal executions  lethal injection executions and Oklahoma  as we talked about on the bonus show  last week using nitrogen gas for these  executions let's go back to why even by  conservative standards as mark Hyden  pointed out to us and made abundantly  clear back in August of 2015 the death  penalty is something that as a developed  Western nation we should be running away  from and slowly but surely we are going  in that direction the question is will  we and if and when we will get to a ban  in all 50 states on the death penalty I  think that there will probably be some  holdouts there my interview with mark  Hyden I'm joined today by mark Hyden  who's the national advocacy coordinator  with conservatives concerned about the  death penalty he comes most recently  from the National Rifle Association  where he was campaign field  representative in the state of Florida  you know mark I think this is probably  the first time on the show that we're  having a former NRA person on because we  just agree with you so much from the  conservative side and I think  increasingly people are understanding  that there's really nothing very  conservative about the death penalty is  there well you're absolutely correct  when you look at the death penalty and  you look at what principles  conservatives hold dear  they just don't line up conservatives  believe in pro-life policies but the  death penalty risks innocent life  conservatives also believe in fiscal  responsibility and restraint but the  death penalty is far more expensive than  life without parole and it's even led to  tax increases and we also believe in  limited government but I don't believe  there's anything limited or wise about  giving an error-prone state the power to  kill its citizens  so I think it's antithetical to  conservative principles and this idea of  the the government having as little  involvement as possible if we sort of  think of the conservative idea of the  government should take the smallest  action possible to have the the best  possible outcome I was recently looking  at a study showing that harsher  sentences including the death penalty do  not actually have a deterrent effect on  crime and do not actually improve the  sort of status quo around crime so why  would the government take what I think  we would all agree is a bigger action  right the putting to death of someone if  we are not even getting that desired  impact of either deterrence or whatever  else it may be well conservatives like  to do cost-benefit analyses and I like  to do the same so I asked myself for a  big broken government program like the  death penalty that has a high human and  fiscal cost what do we get out of this  what's our bang for the buck and  truthfully we don't get much you're  right the death penalty does not deter  murder the state side of the perennial  leaders and executions actually have the  highest murder rates and if you look at  states like New York and New Jersey  their murder rates actually went down  once they repeal the death penalty but  if you go beyond public safety because  we know the death penalty doesn't deter  you also are confronted with the fact  that the system is very harmful on  murder victims friends and family  members because the long drawn-out  process and the incessant media  attention there's another way you know I  read about your position on how the  death penalty fails the families of the  victims I think there's another way to  consider also which is that when you say  well certain types of crimes are worthy  of the death penalty and others that may  appear just as heinous but maybe it  doesn't involve a multiple murder it was  just a single murder doesn't it in a  sense risk devaluing the sort of the the  victims of what has been determined to  be a crime that's not quite bad enough  for the death penalty but bad enough for  life without parole it starts to sort of  categorize the different victims and  affect their families as well it does  and conservatives you know we pride  ourselves on valuing life  but that kind of valuation is  inappropriate it's saying some lives are  worth more than others and that's how  the death penalty works it's very  arbitrary if you look at the the race of  the victim if it's a white person who  may or may not have been murdered by a  person of color there's a higher  likelihood that it's going to turn out  as a death penalty case as opposed to  life without parole and that goes back  to you know Liberty or excuse me Lady  Justice is supposed to be blind but  apparently she's not colorblind so let's  talk a little bit about what you think  as someone who is working within the  conservative world as to why the death  penalty is so appealing to many on the  right at broadly I've talked about this  idea that it that exists on the right  with regard to many social policies of  sort of strict father morality as George  Lakoff has put it which is we're gonna  put aside for a second as you said the  the cost-benefit analyses we're going to  put aside for a second the facts on  deterrence because sometimes we just  have this innate sense that people  deserve to be punished in a way that we  see fit am I on to something there is  there's some other explanation that you  see as to why so many on the right  really like the death penalty I had a  conversation at the North Carolina GOP  convention someone came up to me and  they said you know I support the death  penalty because I think that some people  just deserved to die I said I told them  I'm not gonna argue with that line of  thinking that's your own opinion but do  you trust an error-prone government to  fairly efficiently and properly  administer this program when you don't  trust it to deliver a piece of mail and  the light bulb went off in his head and  he walked away being against the death  penalty we have to look at this program  not in a theoretical framework but how  our government actually operates it and  it operates it in a very very flawed  fashion is that in your mind the best  way speaking as a conservative who has  convinced other conservatives that the  death penalty is a bad idea  is taking a sort of we don't trust the  government approach the best way to  change other conservatives mind on this  because there's so many arguments that  could be made right there's the cost  argument there's the risk of innocent  life argument and we've seen increased  data that suggests one out of twenty  five on death row is not guilty of the  crime they've been convicted of what in  your mind is is the best argument and  maybe that one is it which is listen we  don't trust the government and and we  don't want them putting the death  penalty on anybody  well I found that one to be very  convincing but for me is the  preponderance of the evidence  I used to support the death penalty but  once I looked at it in a conservative  framework and I noticed all of the  problems all of them together is what  pushed me to finally come out against  the death penalty and you know each  person is different some people are led  by the cost arguments which I'm a fiscal  conservative as well but others are not  swayed by that but they're swayed by the  life issues or just their distrust of  government so every person is different  but there's there's no shortage of  reasons to oppose the death penalty  Nebraska is a recent conservative state  that has repealed the death penalty and  your organization played a role there  what is it that you do on the ground to  try to get a repeal going I mean what  are the sort of logistics involved well  it takes years of planning and our  involvement in Nebraska was part of a  broader coalition so there are a lot of  different organizations that were  involved and it went on for years they  were working with legislators persuading  them showing them the information about  the death penalty but they're also  working and I was working as well with  the grass roots and grass tops in  Nebraska to get them to contact their  legislators and let them know which way  they should be voting they should be  voting the way they're conscious they  should be voting the way of their  principles and after years of pushing  towards this Nebraska finally repealed  the death penalty they did so by  overturning a filibuster as well as a  veto override and became the first red  state number forty years to repeal the  death penalty so what can individuals do  who still live in states with the death  penalty is starting with their most  local of legislators the way to go  writing to senators and and us members  of Congress well you know I'm a  conservative and I'd like to think that  politics is best done on a local level  on a state-by-state legislative basis  and that's our focus is to try to repeal  the death penalty at the state level  because that's that's where it's used  the most only three people have been  executed at the federal level in the  modern era so mainly it comes from the  state level especially  these states like Texas so I recommend  you know they contact me go to our  website sign up and also reach out to  their state representatives and state  legislators and start a grassroots  movement in their own local areas is  there some hope even though as you say  most of these decisions are made at the  state level that we could see what  happened with marriage equality happened  with the death penalty which is at a  certain point it becomes very clear that  at the state level there is this  preponderance of desire so to speak  across the country to say no more death  penalty where there might be some sort  of federal or Supreme Court ruling on it  I guess the question would be what kind  of a case could broadly challenge the  death penalty to the supreme court  that's where I see sort of a tough hill  to climb but is there some possibility  of federal action on this I mean there's  always a chance I mean capital cases are  going to the Supreme Court with  regularity and yeah since Justice  Roberts has been put in place I've given  up on trying to figure out which way  they're gonna rule on anything so it's  always a possibility that the Supreme  Court will weigh in recently they looked  at the Glossop case having to do with  the potential of cruel and unusual  punishment and some of the more  left-leaning justices came out and  pretty much opposed to the death penalty  and state of the many reasons why they  can't support it regardless of that  individual case so there's always the  possibility that in the coming years  that a Supreme Court ruling may come  down and in the death penalty nationally  last thing I want to touch on as a  conservative who on this particular  issue sides more with this sort of  prevailing left-leaning opinion in the  US on what other issues do you find  yourself having maybe more natural  allies on the left well you know we're  single issue organizations so I know but  you personally I mean oh me personally  well you know I believe in limited  government and I've and sometimes that  means that I align more with  libertarians as well as some on the left  you know I I there's a multitude of  issues that could come out from that but  when it comes to my voting it's usually  pretty much or it's always on the  Republican line yeah that I'm not at all  surprised to hear all right we've been  speaking with mark Hyden he's the  national advocate  the coordinator with conservatives  concerned about the death penalty thank  you so much for talking to us about this  David thanks for having me on

